{"title":"Social media and political radicalization","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/972407507/0/marginalrevolution~Social-media-and-political-radicalization.html","date":1793870349000,"content":"<p>For a while now, we have been hearing claims that social media radicalize the American electorate, and that social media damage teen mental health.</p>\r\n<p>Well, we just held a major set of elections that produced resoundingly <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://x.com/rsanti97/status/2117805496669282807?s=46&amp;t=fSypFxt84bBHId-do9r5Ag\" target=\"_blank\">moderate results</a>, repudiations of both Trump and the extreme Democratic left.  You may or may not like those results, but they are pretty clearly moderate.</p>\r\n<p>And <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://grimoiremanor.substack.com/p/time-trend-data-now-turning-against?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" target=\"_blank\">the recent trends in teen mental health are positive</a>.  All the while social media usage continues unabated, and furthermore short video continues to rise.</p>\r\n<p>Neither the political nor the mental health trend is guaranteed to continue, but this is a sign that the standard anti-social media polemics are radically incomplete and currently failing in their predictions.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/11/social-media-and-political-radicalization.html\">Social media and political radicalization</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/972407507/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/972407507/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"